# Scope of the exported-doc check (tools/exportdoc; run by hack/apidiff.sh).
#
# the documentation checks asks two things of the exported surface — "every exported
# identifier documents ..." and "`example_test.go` for <workflows>" — and §16
# repeats the first as "every exported declaration has complete GoDoc". This
# file says which module each half applies to, and nothing is skipped
# silently: every line carries a reason, and a `skip` line is printed in the
# report's "NOT CHECKED, and why" block on every run.
#
# Module lines:   <module>  <mode>[,restate-max=N][,fields-min=N]  <reason>
#
#   require  every exported declaration must carry a doc comment
#   report   counted and printed, never a failure
#   skip     not walked at all; the reason is printed
#
#   restate-max=N  ceiling, in per-cent of DOCUMENTED declarations, on comments
#                  that only restate the identifier's name. A ratchet: it is
#                  set at the measured figure plus a comment or two of
#                  headroom, so the number cannot drift upwards unnoticed.
#   fields-min=N   floor, in per-cent, for exported struct fields and
#                  interface methods that carry a comment — including one
#                  documented by its group's or its type's comment, which is
#                  how Go documents a pair of fields. revive's `exported`
#                  rule does not look at fields at all, so this is the half
#                  of the coverage question `make lint` cannot answer.
#
# Why `require` can be green on this tree the day it lands, and what it is
# still for: `.golangci.yml` enables revive's `exported` rule for every
# non-test file, so the 100 % declaration figure was already held by
# `make lint` before this check existed. What is NEW here is
# the field floor, the restatement ceiling, the example table, and the fact
# that every module is named with a mode — revive is silent about a module
# nobody added to the lint run.
#
# Example lines:  example  <§13 workflow>  <pkg:ExampleName,...>  <note>
#
# The workflow column is checked against the documentation checks's own sentence: a
# workflow §13 names and this file does not is a failure, and so is one this
# file names and §13 does not. `-` in the refs column declares a gap, whose
# note is printed as NO EXAMPLE and counted in the summary. `perdriver` asks
# for at least one Example in every package that exports Open.

